# Quantitative Analyst Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

**Run:** Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals
**Author:** Quantitative Analyst (independent Stage 1 brief)
**Date:** 2026-08-06
**Analytical frame:** Reproducible cost, threshold, exposure, and sensitivity
analysis for a quiet-by-design terminal standard at IAD and DCA. All inputs
are cited to the airport context packet or to primary technical standards.
Analyst constructions (formulas, scenario labels, per-enplanement math) are
labeled as such. Reproducible files sit in
`outputs/stage1/quantitative-analysis/`.

---

## Executive summary

The economics of a quiet-by-design standard, taken at face value, do not
justify delay. An MWAA acoustic, public-address (PA), voice-alarm, AV, and
FIDS package priced against Concourse E scales (435,000 gross square feet,
opening autumn 2026) lands between roughly **$3.5 million and $21.3 million
of one-time capital across low-, mid-, and high-rigor scenarios**, or
**$0.015 to $0.093 per enplanement per year** when amortized over 30-year
revenue debt at MWAA's recent 4.37% coupon. Against a Dulles cost per enplanement in the mid-single-
dollar range and a $20–22.5 billion expanded program envelope, the standard
is a rounding error in the CIP — and a large multiple of that number if it is
retrofitted later. Published integrated-project-delivery research puts the
cost of resolving a design decision during construction at roughly 4× to 10×
the cost of resolving it during schematic design, and 20× or more during
operations. The right question is not "can MWAA afford this standard" but
"what does the standard have to specify so that Concourse E, the C/D
replacement, and any DCA Terminal 1 rebuild inherit it automatically?"

Five numerical anchors organize the argument:

1. **NFPA 72 already fixes the intelligibility floor.** Emergency voice/alarm
   communications systems (EVACS) must achieve a Speech Transmission Index
   (STI) of at least 0.50 in areas where voice messaging is required. This is
   the non-negotiable minimum any MWAA standard must meet or exceed; it is
   not aspirational.
2. **The acoustic/PA/AV/EVACS package is a small share of a terminal.** A
   defensible range from public benchmark literature is 1.0%–3.5% of hard
   terminal construction cost. At the Concourse E scale, the delta between a
   low-rigor and a high-rigor MWAA standard is roughly $18 million of one-
   time capital.
3. **Late change is the real cost.** Applying published MacLeamy-curve
   multipliers, changing an acoustic or PA-zoning specification during CD/
   construction on a $10 million package can cost $40–100 million in delay,
   rework, and integration; changing it after commissioning can exceed
   $200 million-equivalent when displacement of operations is priced in.
4. **Announcement volume is a compoundable load.** A 14-gate concourse
   operating 16 hours per day with a conservative average of 15 announcements
   per gate per hour broadcasts roughly 3,360 audible events per day. Zoning
   the PA to a single gate holdroom collapses the *listener-weighted*
   exposure by roughly one order of magnitude for the same operational
   information content.
5. **Accessibility redundancy is a coverage math problem, not a policy
   preference.** ADA/ABA and DOT 49 CFR Part 27 do not permit "quiet" to
   mean "no channel." The standard has to guarantee three concurrent channels
   (visual, audible, staffed/mobile) at every gate and every safety-critical
   decision point. Any decision to reduce audible frequency must be met with
   measurable increases in the other two channels.

The following sections show the arithmetic.

