## 3. Intelligibility thresholds the MWAA standard has to meet

A quiet-by-design standard is not a marketing target; it is a set of
measurable numbers that a commissioning agent can verify. The following
thresholds are drawn from primary technical standards and should be adopted
verbatim or improved upon by MWAA:

| Metric | Governing standard | Threshold | Applicability |
|--------|--------------------|-----------|---------------|
| Speech Transmission Index (STI), EVACS-required areas | NFPA 72 (2022) §18.4.11.2, Common Intelligibility Scale/STI | ≥ 0.50 (measurement per IEC 60268-16) | All concourses, holdrooms, restrooms, connectors — everywhere emergency voice messaging is required |
| Ambient A-weighted sound level, occupied concourse | Airport design practice; ASHRAE Applications; ISO 9921 | Design target ≤ 55–60 dB(A) during typical peak (analyst benchmark) | Airside holdrooms, connectors |
| Reverberation time (T60), broadband | ANSI/ASA S12.60-2010 (schools) as pattern; airport design practice | Design target 1.2–1.6 s in large volumes; ≤ 0.8 s in gate hold pockets (analyst benchmark) | Large-volume terminal spaces vs. localized holdrooms |
| Signal-to-noise ratio during PA broadcast | IEC 60268-16 | ≥ +15 dB above ambient for intelligibility | All PA-broadcast zones |
| Adjacent-zone spillover from a paged zone | Airport design practice; no fixed standard | Analyst target: ≤ −10 dB relative to the paged zone | Zoned PA implementations |
| Assistive listening coverage | ADA Standards for Accessible Design §219, §706 | Required in assembly areas; specific receiver counts by capacity | All assembly/holdroom areas ≥50 occupants |
| Visual message coverage | 49 CFR Part 27; DOT ADA Air Carrier rule (14 CFR Part 382) analogue for terminal | Visual counterpart to every safety-critical audible message | All public areas |

Two implications:

1. **STI ≥ 0.50 is a floor, not a target.** Any quiet-by-design language that
   permits ambiguity here is out of compliance with NFPA 72 as adopted
   through local building codes. The standard should specify STI ≥ 0.55 as a
   design target with ≥ 0.50 as the acceptance minimum, and require
   measurement by a third-party commissioning agent per IEC 60268-16 in
   every EVACS zone at both handover and every three years thereafter.
2. **Ambient and reverberation are analyst benchmarks, not codes.** MWAA
   should specify them explicitly because no code will do it. The analyst
   ranges above are drawn from ASHRAE and school-acoustics literature by
   analogy; MWAA's design manual should either adopt them or replace them
   with values derived from its own commissioning data. This is a gap the
   operator has to close.

