## 6. Compliance measurement cadence — what a defensible standard costs
   to *verify*

A standard is only real if someone measures it. Analyst-recommended
verification cadence for the MWAA standard, with rough labor estimates:

| Verification | Cadence | Rough effort per event |
|--------------|--------:|-----------------------:|
| STI acceptance test, every EVACS zone | Once at commissioning, then every 3 years | 8–16 person-hours per zone with calibrated equipment |
| Ambient LAeq / reverberation survey | Every 3 years and after any major fit-out | 24–40 person-hours per concourse |
| PA zone spillover audit | Every 3 years | 16–24 person-hours per concourse |
| FIDS uptime and coverage audit | Continuous (systems monitoring) + annual physical audit | 40–80 person-hours per airport per year |
| Assistive-listening receiver check | Quarterly | 4–8 person-hours per airport per quarter |
| Passenger survey (perceived quietness, intelligibility) | Annual | Existing MWAA survey infrastructure |

At $200/hour blended cost for third-party commissioning agents (analyst
assumption; typical U.S. specialty-acoustics consulting range $150–$300/hr),
system-wide verification costs order-of-magnitude **$150K–$300K/year across
both airports** — a rounding error against the CIP and against the
$1.3 M/year debt-service on the high-rigor package. Verification cost is
not the reason to skip the standard.

